# =============================================================================
# itera-ex-air-scalar-buffers — AIR-lane scalar bounded buffer iteration.
# =============================================================================
#
# What this teaches:
#   • a full literal `list<b8, size N>` / `ascii<size N>` / `string<size N>` proves len == N, so
#     `for from` unrolls guard-free on the AIR lane (N lanes against the
#     per-function unroll budget of 64)
#   • each lane binds the scalar index element row — `list<b8, size N>[i]` is a
#     `b8`, `ascii<size N>[i]` / `string<size N>[i]` is `ascii<size 1>` — and a full
#     literal only reads its own slots, so the non-ASCII trap row can never
#     fire from a dead slot
#   • a runtime-length buffer (a parameter) keeps declining causa=
#     not_concrete; spare slots past len are never read
#
# Common mistakes:
#   • expecting a runtime-length buffer to unroll — only a proven-full literal
#     erases the loop
#   • capacities past 64 lanes — the AIR budget declines the whole loop
#
# See also: list<b8, size N>, ascii, string, itera-ex-scalar-buffers
# =============================================================================

# Route A full-literal drivers. The itera loop itself is admitted (the
# semantic unit tests in air_unroll_test.rs pin the emitted lanes, the
# guard-free shape and the element types), but each bare `↦ list<b8, size 8>` /
# `↦ ascii<size 8>` / `↦ string<size 8>` conversion is a boundary throw that the AIR
# lane declines with SEM059 runtime_conversion (air_purity.rs, ATH-3), so
# `faber check` rejects this file: three runtime_conversion errors, one per driver.

@ radix lane "air"
fn full_bytes() → void {
    let list<b8, size 8> xs ← |de ad be ef ba dc fe ca| ↦ list<b8, size 8>
    for from xs let b { }
}

@ radix lane "air"
fn full_ascii() → void {
    let ascii<size 8> xs ← 'alphabet' ↦ ascii<size 8>
    for from xs let c { }
}

@ radix lane "air"
fn full_string() → void {
    let string<size 8> xs ← "abcdefgh" ↦ string<size 8>
    for from xs let c { }
}
